.TH skeeper 1  "skeeper 0.1.0" "Skeeper Manual"
.SH NAME
skeeper \- Simple terminal session keeper
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBskeeper\fR [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fIsubcommands\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Simple terminal session keeper
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version and exit
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.SS skeeper-new
Create a new session and attach to it
.PP
\fBskeeper\-new\fR [\fB\-d\fR|\fB\-\-detached\fR] [\fB\-s\fR|\fB\-\-shell\fR] [\fB\-c\fR|\fB\-\-cwd\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fINAME\fR] 
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-detached\fR
Create only, do not attach
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-shell\fR \fI<SHELL>\fR
Shell to run inside the session
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-cwd\fR \fI<CWD>\fR
Initial working directory (default: current directory)
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
[\fINAME\fR]
Session name (a random name is generated if omitted)
.SS skeeper-attach
Attach to a session
.PP
\fBskeeper\-attach\fR [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fINAME\fR] 
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
[\fINAME\fR]
Session name to attach to (opens an interactive picker if omitted)
.SS skeeper-list
List all sessions
.PP
\fBskeeper\-list\fR [\fB\-d\fR|\fB\-\-detail\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-detail\fR
Show detail sub\-table for each attached client (pid, tty, ssh, attach time)
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.SS skeeper-detach
Detach from the current session
.PP
\fBskeeper\-detach\fR [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.SS skeeper-rename
Rename a session
.PP
\fBskeeper\-rename\fR [\fB\-o\fR|\fB\-\-old\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fINEW_NAME\fR> 
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-old\fR \fI<OLD>\fR
Rename the session with this name (default: the current one)
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
<\fINEW_NAME\fR>
New session name
.SS skeeper-kill
Kill (destroy) a session
.PP
\fBskeeper\-kill\fR [\fB\-a\fR|\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fINAME\fR] 
.TP
\fB\-a\fR, \fB\-\-all\fR
Kill all sessions
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Skip confirmation prompt
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
[\fINAME\fR]
Session name to kill (default: the current one)
.SS skeeper-prune
Prune orphan session files (server crashed or otherwise dead)
.PP
\fBskeeper\-prune\fR [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Create a new session and attach to it:
.EX
skeeper new myproject
.EE
.TP
Create a detached session (do not attach):
.EX
skeeper new -d myproject
.EE
.TP
Attach to an existing session (or open a picker if the name is omitted):
.EX
skeeper attach myproject
.EE
.TP
List sessions, with per-client detail (tty, SSH, attach time):
.EX
skeeper list --detail
.EE
.TP
Detach from the current session (leave the daemon running):
.EX
skeeper detach
.EE
.TP
Kill a session by name and clean up its files:
.EX
skeeper kill myproject
.EE
.TP
Clean up orphan session files after a server crash:
.EX
skeeper prune
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
\fI$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/skeeper/\fR
Runtime directory for session state, created with mode 0700. Falls back to \fI$HOME/.skeeper/run/\fR when \fBXDG_RUNTIME_DIR\fR is unset.
.TP
\fI<uuid>.json\fR
Session metadata: name, cwd, shell, server pid, attached client list. Written atomically via rename(2).
.TP
\fI<uuid>.sock\fR
Unix domain socket that carries pty data between the server and its attached clients.
.TP
\fI<uuid>.ctl\fR
Unix domain socket for control messages (detach request, rename, session switch).
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBtmux\fR(1), \fBscreen\fR(1), \fBabduco\fR(1), \fBdtach\fR(1)
.SH VERSION
v0.1.0
